<sect> window (Revision 1.18 -- 08/09/99)

<p>This section is only in the process of being written, don't look at it
as complete!

The unit <tt/window/ provides functions to process a time-sorted
file. An arbitrary number of consecutive data records can be picked
out (data window) and stored in the matrix <tt/m/ for further calculations.

<sect1> Window Data Structure

<sect2> struct data_window
<label id="stuct-data-window">
<p>Structure to hold file pointer, matrix pointer, time values, etc.
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
struct data_window {
        double mid_time;        /* mid time of actual window */
        double tail_time;       /* start time of actual window */
        double head_time;       /* end time of actual window */
        double halfwinsize;     /* deltatime = (endtime - starttime)/2 */
        int bufsize;            /* maximal number of rows for a window */
        int valid_count;        /* actual number of rows */
        FILE *fpdata;           /* file pointer to data file */
        char *fname;            /* file name of data file */
        int ispipe;             /* flag whether fpdata is a pipe */
        struct matrix *m;       /* struct containing the data */
</verb></tscreen>

<p>The variables, which must be filled up, are explained later on.

<sect1>Window functions
<sect2>init_data_window
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int init_data_window(
        const double half_window_size,
        const int buffer_size,
        const int numcols,
        const char *datafname,
        struct data_window *w,
        int (*read_new_element)(FILE *fpin, struct matrix *m, int row))
</verb></tscreen>
<p>
The variable <tt/half_window_size/ defines the size of the window,
concretely twice the specified value. The unit of the specified value
must be the same as in the data file, e.g. if the data file is sorted
by seconds, the unit for <tt/half_window_size/ must also be seconds.
The variables <tt/buffer_size/ and <tt/numcols/ define the size of the
<tt/m/-Matrix. The variable <tt/buffer_size/ holds the number of rows,
which is some more than the number of data records in the spezified
window, and the variable <tt/numcols/ holds the number of columns,
which is the number of different parts of one data record. The
variable <tt/datafname/ contains the name of the data file.
If the initialization was successful, the return value is 0, any other
return value can be handed over to the <tt/window_err/ function, which
prompts an appropriate error message.

<sect2>update_data_window
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int update_data_window(
        struct data_window *w,
        const double new_time,
        int (*read_new_element)(FILE *fpin, struct matrix *m, int row))
 </verb></tscreen>
<p>    
This function moves the center of the data window on to <tt/new_time/,
thus the data window extends now from <tt/new_time/ - <tt/half_window-size/ to
<tt/new_time/ + <tt/half_window_size/. The return value contains the
number of data records, which the window was shifted, so a return value
greater or equal zero is normal, a return value smaller zero must be
handed over to the <tt/window_err/ function, as it indicates, that
something went wrong.


<sect2>eliminate_data_window
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
void eliminate_data_window (struct data_window *w)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>   
This function eliminates the window structure and frees the allocated memory.

<sect2>time_index_data_window
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int time_index_data_window(struct data_window *w, double t)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>   
This function returns the rownumber of the matrix <tt/m/, which
corresponds to the time <tt/t/.

<sect2>print_data_window
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
void print_data_window (FILE *fpout, struct data_window *w, int fmt1, int fmt2)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>   
This function prints the current values of <tt/mid_time/,
<tt/tail_time/, <tt/head_time/, <tt/halfwinsize/, <tt/bufsize/, and
<tt/valid_count/ of the <tt/data_window/. This may be useful for
debugging.

<sect2>window_err
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
void window_err (const int errval, const char* fname)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>   
This function prompts an appropriate error message to <tt/stderr/ for
all unusual return values produced by the window functions and stops
the program. 
